{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>The daughter of a Canaanite man.</b> Onkelos says (Onkelos on Genesis 38:2) תגרא, that is, a merchant who came to sojourn in the land for his trade. His intent is to say that the sons of Jacob took care not to marry Canaanite women, as their father Isaac commanded them, and Abraham likewise, and so they stated in the Gemara in tractate Pesachim (Pesachim 50a). And their wives were Egyptians, Ammonites, Moabites, and women of distinguished lineage from the sons of Ishmael and the sons of Keturah. Therefore Scripture singled out concerning Saul the son of Simeon (below 46:10), \"and Saul the son of the Canaanite woman,\" for he alone among them was such, and there too they interpreted (Bereshit Rabbah 80:11) that she was Dinah, who had been violated by a Canaanite. And our Sages (Bereshit Rabbah 84:19) disagreed in the matter: they said, Rabbi Yehudah says that twin sisters were born with the tribes and they married them. Rabbi Nechemiah says, they were Canaanite women. And it is possible that Rabbi Nechemiah did not insist on their lineage, for he intended only to say that they married women of the land of Canaan, but they were from the converts and sojourners who came from all the lands, or Ammonites and Moabites and from the rest of the nations, for he intended only to dispute Rabbi Yehudah's position that they did not marry their sisters, since a maternal sister is forbidden to the children of Noah (Sanhedrin 58a). And according to the opinion of Rabbi Yehudah, the sons of Leah would need to marry the twins of the other six, and they would marry theirs; or perhaps Rabbi Nechemiah does not concede the existence of twins at all, and there was no daughter other than Dinah, as the verse implies. And according to reason, it is not proper that they all married Canaanite women, for then the inheritors of the land would be from the seed of Canaan the cursed servant, just as from the seed of Abraham — and Scripture commanded to devote them to destruction and that there shall be no remnant or survivor left to him. In any case this man was a merchant, for why would Scripture need to say that he was a Canaanite by lineage, when all the people of that land are Canaanites — from the Perizzite and the Jebusite and their brethren, for they all trace their descent to Canaan, and their dwelling is in the land of Canaan? It would have been fitting to say, \"and Judah took there a wife, and her name was such,\" as Scripture mentioned the names of the women — Tamar, and the wives of Esau, and others (above 4:19, 11:29, 25:1). Rather, it explained about him that he was a merchant, that he was not of the land that belonged to the Hivite or the Amorite. And this is the reason for \"and Judah saw there the daughter of a Canaanite man\" — because of her father he married her. And the verse that says, \"The sons of Judah: Er and Onan and Shelah; three were born to him from Bat-Shua the Canaanite woman\" (1 Chronicles 2:3) — because she was the daughter of the Canaanite man she was so called, for that man was called by them the merchant, for he was known and distinguished by the trade on account of which he sojourned there. And Rabbi Avraham said (below 46:10), that because this woman was a Canaanite and transgressed the will of his fathers, her sons were wicked and died, and therefore Scripture mentioned it; and it mentioned Saul alone as the son of the Canaanite woman, for there was no need to mention this concerning Shelah. And if so, the sense of \"and Judah saw there\" would be that he saw her and desired her, as in the matter of \"and he saw a woman in Timnah\" regarding Samson (Judges 14:1). And in the portion of \"and Jacob lived\" (Rashi on Genesis 50:13), Rashi of blessed memory wrote: \"and his sons carried him\" and not his grandsons, for thus he commanded them: let no one of your sons carry me in my bed, because they are from daughters of Canaan (Bamidbar Rabbah 2:8). And this would be because Saul the son of Simeon and Shelah the son of Judah were from daughters of Canaan, and therefore he would exclude all of them. But in the versions of Bereshit Rabbah that we have, we find in all of them: \"one of your sons\" — who among them has daughters of Canaan. And likewise Tamar was the daughter of one of the converts in the land, not the daughter of a Canaanite by lineage, for heaven forbid that our master David and our righteous Messiah, who will be revealed to us speedily, should be from the seed of Canaan the cursed servant. And our Sages said (Bereshit Rabbah 85:10) concerning Tamar that she was the daughter of Shem, and he was priest to El Elyon."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 38, :notes {0 "Rabbi Avraham refers to Ibn Ezra. The Rashi citation appears to reference Genesis 49:29 rather than 50:13."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:41:48.938720856Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}